Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||12-29-1999||09:34 PM||||yay! I finally installed a new clutch master cylinder, and my shifting in my '86 4spd. 2m6 SE has gotten much easier. my question is this: all my gearshifts are great now, except for reverse. all i get when trying for reverse is grinding... whadda i do? someone please help me out- i HAVE bled the system and put in dexron mercon (sp?) ATF. any help is great! i would be willing to pay up to 75 dollars to anyone who would be willing to come to my house in oceanside, CA, and help me out, if it isnt something i can do myself.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Phil||12-29-1999||09:58 PM||||Have you adjusted your linkage? The Haynes manual has the procedure, it's quite simple.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||12-29-1999||11:40 PM||||Do you have an aluminun clutch pedal. If so chances are its bent. then it wont disengage the clutch enough. You need the steel one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||12-30-1999||12:20 AM||||The unfortuante thing about reverse is that Fiero trannies don't have a synchronizer for reverse (at least for the 4 speeds). It is a good idea to follow the others advise but I have never heard of a fix for the reverse grind. If you try reverse too fast it will grind. Now I don't recall that problem with the 5 speeds but Im not really sure.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-30-1999||12:43 AM||||I don't recall the shift pattern for the 4 speeds, but if you have a gear directly opposite reverse, try shifting into that 1st then into reverse.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroSTETZ||12-30-1999||12:54 AM||||the car has done it for as long as i have owned it, about 5 months... i have the chilton's manual, is this nearly the same as the haynes? the chiltons is rather brief, could anyone help me w/ clarification<P>the shift pattern is like so=<P>r 1 3<BR>|_||<BR>  | |<BR>  2 4||||reg||1
Z||000006||Thomas||12-30-1999||05:28 AM||||Have you bleeding the system like show in the manuel ?<BR>I have tehe same car, with the same problem after slave and master cylinder swaping.<BR>Then i bleed the system under pressure with a break bleeding tool. -- No noise at shifting in reverse. A little bit of air was in system.<BR>Another possibility is the pedal. At 86 Fieros somtimes the metal from the bolt, where the rod from the Master cylinder sits on is grinding off. So the rod can´t move the full way. <BR>DON´T USE A LONGER ROD FOR THE SLAVE CYLINDER TO THE CLUTCH LEVER.<BR>Or some weeks later the finger of your pessure plate cracks.<BR>I have all this behind me.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||12-30-1999||05:43 AM||||Stetz:<BR>I have an isuzu 4 speed and the pattern is a little different<BR>R 1 3<BR>| ||<BR>_ 2 4 <P>I sometimes get a grind but I think as stated above it is from R not being synchro'ed<P>If I just let in on the clutch for a few secs before trying to shift at all then I dont have the problem.  <P>What if you try to start it in Reverse?  Does the car try to back up with the clutch depressed and it in reverse?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 12-30-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000008||Thomas||12-30-1999||11:02 AM||||A Fiero memorial second before shift in reverse is normal for shifting without noises. But noises after three seconds is not normal.||||reg||6
Z||000009||Scott2M4||12-30-1999||12:00 PM||||I have a feeling STETZ's shift pattern is the same (the forum software likes to eat leading and trailing spaces for some reason - thus shifting his diagram over to the left edge).  I'm pretty sure ALL Fiero 4-speeds have the same pattern (1st above 2nd, 3rd above 4th and reverse over to the left and up).  Am I incorrect?<P>I've always found shifting into 2nd before reverse prevents any grinding.  YMMV||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroSTETZ||12-30-1999||03:02 PM||||scotts right (thanks scott) so theres no sure fix to totally eliminate grinding? (on isuzu 4spd?) what about me taking it to aamco or some other tranny shop? 20 bucks to check it out too much?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||12-30-1999||03:39 PM||||I believe that the 4 sps are by Muncie and only the 5sp 4cly cars had Izuzu 5sp V6 are Getrag. I'm sure I will be corrected if wrong||||reg||1
Z||000012||87GTBro||12-30-1999||03:41 PM||||I have a Pontiac Sunbird GT 4sp with the same shift pattern.  If I push in on the clutch and immediately jam the stick into reverse, it grinds the heck out of the gears.  But if I push in on the clutch and wait a few seconds to put it in reverse, there is no problem.  I sense that the gears or clutch plate or whatever it is called has to stop spinning to keep from grinding.<P>I also have an 86 GT with 4sp trans.  It does the same thing.||||reg||1
