Z||000014||FieroSTETZ||12-31-1999||04:54 PM||||well, i have replaced the master cylinder, as i told you all, but i have noticed that sometimes my shifting is buttery smooht, others it is very notchy and difficult... could this be because of me using ATF? also, it looked like the shifter rod shaft thing that goes from the cylinder to the clutch pedal is shorter... could this cause it to disengage fully sometimes, and not full yother times? none of the tricks anyone has posted have worked so far (2nd, then reverse, third then reverse, waiting a while witth the clutch down) there IS a place by me that will sell me an adjustable threaded clutch pedal rod thing... is it possible that my clutch doesnt push in far enough? if so, would this be a good idea?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Greg Piet||12-31-1999||05:24 PM||||I had a similiar problem with the shifting sometimes good, sometimes impossible.  I rebuilt the master, no significant change, did the thing to the pedal with little/no change.  When i replaced the slave cylinder all was fixed.  I didnt see on here that you replaced the slave cylinder.  That part would be my guess.||||reg||1
Z||000016||862M4inCA||01-01-2000||03:46 PM||||This may or may not help with the four speed tranny, however in my 5 speed I too get a little grinding action when shifting into reverse, and have always found that if I put it into first and then into reverse I don't get the grinding noise.  I had a friend a while back with the same problem in her '91 Mustang, it ALWAYS grinded in reverse. We decided to put a new clutch kit in and never heard another grind. Might be something to look into. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000017||862M4inCA||01-01-2000||03:49 PM||||I might want to add that I've never had any shifting problems with mine except that annoying reverse grind. All of my forward gears shift smoothly and easily. Though I do think that soon I'll need to replace my clutch disk since I don't have to press my clutch very far to shift from one gear to another. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||FieroSTETZ||01-02-2000||12:23 AM||||hmm... since it does not feel like it is disengaging all the way, it could be the slave cylinder? how could i go about checking? how much is a new or refurb slave cylinder? can it be rebuilt?||||reg||1
Z||000019||fiero56||01-02-2000||12:39 AM||||To check the slave have someone push and hold down the clutch pedal while you watch the rod coming from the slave. If it slowly creeps in, you need new seals at the least. Rebuild kits are $30? New slaves run a good $120 or more from a dealer, 'bout a hundred from autozone or similar. Rebuild kits can be a cheaper solution IF they work. If not, you end up buying a new slave on top of what you already payed for the rebuild. I bought a new one. Don't forget about the master cylinder. It could be bad.||||reg||1
